	affiliations = {Department of Sociology and Center for Demography and Ecology, University of Wisconsin-Madison, Madison, WI, United States},
	abstract = {In addition to traditional attributes like income, wealth, and education, non-economic family characteristics such as time resources, expectations, and social and cultural capital are central in influencing social mobility. Applying core sociological theories of cultural transmission, concerted cultivation, and status attainment to the case of teaching occupations, we investigate whether the children of educators experience greater upward mobility net of traditional determinants of status attainment. While educators typically hold moderate incomes compared with other similarly high-educated professionals, we theorize that their formal training in child development and academic knowledge, enhanced cultural capital, compatible work and leave schedules with children, and raised awareness and ability to evaluate the effectiveness of cultivation strategies and opportunities intersect to provide optimal circumstances for their children's educational and income mobility in adulthood. Utilizing a combination of classical relative mobility and contemporary causal decomposition strategies with long-standing household-level longitudinal surveys in the United States (PSID) and United Kingdom (BHPS/UKHLS), we observe higher income attainment for the children of primary/secondary educators, predominantly driven by increased educational attainment. These results emphasize the importance of occupations in capturing indirect non-economic assets to the status attainment process. We discuss implications for alleviating teacher shortages in the U.S. and U.K. © 2026},
