	affiliations = {Li Anmin Institute of Economic Research, Faculty of Economics, Liaoning University, No. 66, Chongshan Middle Road, Huanggu District, Liaoning Province, Shenyang City, China},
	abstract = {This paper investigates how city size shapes intergenerational income mobility in urban China. Using nationally representative panel data, we find that larger cities exhibit lower mobility, with disadvantaged children experiencing reduced upward mobility (a “sticky floor”) and advantaged children less prone to downward mobility (a “sticky ceiling”). We develop a theoretical framework in which city size affects human capital investment through polarized skill returns. Empirical results support this mechanism: labor market structures in large cities amplify the influence of family background, leading to greater inequality in economic outcomes across generations. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
