	pages = {94 - 108},
	doi = {10.61211/mjqr120107},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105040529117?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Cebu Technological University-Naga Extension Campus, Central Poblacion, Naga, 6037, Philippines},
	abstract = {Education is the bedrock of sustainable development. It is widely recognized as a critical driver of social mobility, particularly for marginalized populations such as first-generation university students (FGUS). Providing opportunities for these marginalized groups will, as an indispensable tool, help transform individuals and communities at large. Using the snowball sampling technique, this qualitative study examined the lived experiences of eleven selected FGUS at a Philippine State University, employing semi-structured interviews and focus group discussions to explore how students negotiate their academic trajectories amid personal limitations and institutional demands. The thematic analysis identified three recurring themes: familial dedication, structural hurdles, and steadfastness. Findings showed that despite notable socio-economic and technological impediments, such as financial constraints and a lack of devices, these students succeeded due to their intrinsic motivation and familial obligation. For many participants, persistence was constructed not merely as an individual achievement but as a collective investment in intergenerational mobility. The analysis further demonstrated the dynamic interplay between structural conditions and personal agency in shaping the FGUS’s academic engagement and continuity. Institutional factors such as the availability of financial support mechanisms, academic flexibility, and student support services emerged as contextual influences that affected their ability to remain steadfast and motivated. The investigation underscores the need for open, impartial higher education to sustain FGUS's success and urges university administrators to provide holistic support, including financial aid, mental health services, and flexible work-study programs. Hence, this study requires interdisciplinary responses that integrate personal narratives into institutional policies and support systems that empower individuals and communities toward sustainable futures, breaking the intergenerational cycles of indigence. © 2026, Qualitative Research Association of Malaysia (QRAM). All rights reserved.},
