	abstract = {We examine the relationship between farm size and productivity during India's Green Revolution, a period of rapid technological transformation. Using a unique panel of over 5000 Indian farm households that spans the Green Revolution (1971–1999), we show that the classic (linear) inverse farm size–productivity relationship gradually evolved into the U-shaped pattern observed today in India, with both small and large farms outperforming mid-sized ones, by the turn of the century. The roll-out of high-yielding varieties seems to have driven this change, and also driven the increased rate of hired labor use and mechanization on large farms (relative to mid-sized farms) that underpins that enhanced large farm productivity. Last, we show that the Green Revolution may have reduced the propensity of the smallest farms to grow into medium-sized farms over time. © 2025 The Author(s). Applied Economic Perspectives and Policy published by Wiley Periodicals LLC on behalf of Agricultural & Applied Economics Association.},
