	affiliations = {Département des sciences économiques, Université du Québec à Montréal, Montréal, QC, Canada},
	abstract = {Canada has consistently been estimated to be more socio-economically mobile than the United States overall, but the greater steepness of its rank-rank relationship below the 20th percentile of the parental income distribution has thus far remained unexplained. In this study, we use recent linkages between the Intergenerational Income Database (IID) and census data to resolve this puzzle. We show that the non-linearity in the rank-rank relationship observed at the bottom of the parental income distribution is almost entirely attributable to the very low adulthood incomes of Indigenous children who grew up in low-income families. We find that mobility is about two times lower for children from Indigenous backgrounds. While tax exemption for income earned on Indigenous reserve may partially account for the low levels of income observed in tax data, other factors may also be at play that warrant future investigation to better understand the causes and consequences of this intergenerational dynamic. We strongly encourage Statistics Canada to continue to update the IID by adding tax years as their data become available, integrating new birth cohorts, and linking to additional data. © Canadian Public Policy/Analyse de politiques, 2026.},
