	affiliations = {Department of Economics, Indian Institute of Technology Bombay, Mumbai, 400076, India},
	abstract = {Parental time is a crucial and often irreplaceable input in a child’s human capital formation. The pattern of parental time investment across economic and educational backgrounds determines intergenerational mobility in society. Like many developing countries, India is experiencing persistent intergenerational economic and educational immobility. Given this background, this is the first-ever attempt to understand the determinants of parental time allocation for children’s learning in Indian nuclear families, using the Indian Time Use Survey data for 2019 and 2024. Based on the bi-Tobit regression analysis, the study finds that parents with higher economic and educational levels spend more time on their children’s learning than those with lower levels. This result suggests the persistence of education and economic status across generations in Indian nuclear families. The findings also reveal that higher school facilities induce parents to invest more time in children’s learning due to complementarity. However, the interaction between parental socio-economic status and school facilities reveals heterogeneity in parental involvement; mothers’ time spent on children’s learning increases as their education and economic status improve, meaning that better school facilities complement mothers from high socio-economic status. In contrast, fathers’ involvement decreases, highlighting opportunity cost and gendered role specialisation in the household. Thus, improvements in school facilities alone may not be enough to increase parental time devoted to children’s learning, particularly among poorer families. Complementary measures, such as income support, more employment opportunities, greater parental awareness program, and stronger parental engagement in school activities, are important for enabling poorer families to participate more in children’s education. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to The Indian Econometric Society 2026.},
