	abstract = {This study aims to provide a comprehensive comparative overview of the implementation of digital ecosystems in lifelong learning applied by CLCs in Japan (Kominkan) and Indonesia (Pusat Kegiatan Belajar Masyarakat or PKBM). This research employed a mixed-methods approach with a sequential explanatory design. The quantitative phase involved a comparative descriptive analysis of 30 PKBMs in Indonesia and 30 CLCs in Japan using saturated sampling. The data included institutional profiles and learning evaluations analyzed via t tests and normality tests. Through thematic analysis of focus group discussions (FGDs) and semi structured interviews with three academicians and representatives from four specific institutions in Sendai, Kawanishi, and Semarang, the qualitative phase provided a deeper context. Results: Quantitative findings from Indonesia demonstrated that digital interventions significantly improved management competency, with average scores increasing from 65.76 to 89.55 (a 36.18 increase) and a significant t-count of 10.648 ( p < 0.05 ). Qualitatively, the study revealed a contrast in digital urgency: Indonesian PKBMs show a high dependency on digital ecosystems for equivalency programs and vocational skills (e-commerce), driven by national standards and market needs. In contrast, Japanese Kominkans prioritize face-to-face interaction to foster social cohesion and "learning to live together," utilizing digital tools primarily as a supplementary medium for discussing local issues. The digital ecosystem acts as a competency accelerator in Indonesia and a social alternative in Japan. While PKBM excels in digital adaptivity for economic mobility, Kominkan excels in preserving social values and community autonomy. This study implies that for lifelong learning to thrive, CLCs must balance technological integration with human-centric social education, ensuring that infrastructure is supported by competent resources and internet safety protocols. Copyrig