	affiliations = {Department of Family and Consumer Sciences, University of Ghana, Legon, Accra, Ghana},
	abstract = {Rental housing increasingly plays a central role in addressing urban housing demand in Ghana. This study examines the economic and social benefits of rental housing for tenants and landlords in the Okaikoi South Sub-Metropolitan Area of Accra. Adopting a cross-sectional mixed-methods design, data were collected from 250 tenants and 50 landlords across five neighbourhoods in Accra. Quantitative data were analysed using descriptive statistics, factor analysis, and chi-square tests, while qualitative data underwent thematic analysis. The findings identified two broad benefit domains: economic (affordability and income generation) and social (informal childcare among co-tenants, safety and security, social interaction, and reduced loneliness). Specifically, 94% of tenants spent no more than 30% of their income on rent, 71% earned below GHS 1000 per month, 53% were self-employed, and 90% were migrants. Most landlords were older adults (75% aged over 40), with 38% being non-indigenes. Proximity to the workplace emerged as the most influential factor in tenants' choice of rental accommodation. Participants also indicated that renting represents a lifestyle choice and facilitates impression management. Despite the global challenges associated with renting, particularly in many Western countries, the predominantly informal nature of Ghana's economy and the cultural norms governing rental relationships enable most tenants and landlords to cooperate amicably, allowing tenants to remain in their homes until they choose to relocate. These findings demonstrate that rental housing in the study area extends beyond serving as a temporary option; it promotes livelihoods, social integration, and economic mobility. The study concludes by highlighting implications for housing policy, urban planning, and future research. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
