	affiliations = {Oslo Business School at Oslo Metropolitan University, Norway; Department of Economic History, Lund University, Sweden},
	abstract = {How reliable is historical microdata? Understanding historical labor force participation is crucial for assessing long-term trends in economic development and intergenerational mobility. Yet most historical studies focus on men, and little is known about the reliability of quantitative historical sources for measuring women’s work. This paper documents that the measurement of women’s economic activity in the 1910 Norwegian population census has a high level of consistency with other official sources. The original census reports document careful attention to how economic activity was classified. Aggregating the micro data reproduces historical census tables with high accuracy, and contemporary industrial censuses and tax statistics confirm the results found in the census. In addition, a double-enumeration feature of the Norwegian census is leveraged to assess consistency between enumerators, finding no indication that precision in the occupational classification of women is any lower than for men. Some potential sources of downward bias are found in the historical census microdata set provided by IPUMS. Based on the results in this paper, the census data appears well suited to study economic activity using modern econometric methods, for women as well as men. However, limitations due to contemporary reporting practices must be kept in mind. A small upward revision of the 39% female labor force participation in Norway in 1910 might be in order. Similar enumeration practices across countries mean that the approaches introduced here are relevant for other historical censuses from the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries. © 2026 The Author.},
