	affiliations = {University of Georgia, United States; Washington University in St. Louis, United States},
	abstract = {Objective: Economic mobility is a critical yet understudied dimension of refugee integration. This study examines which factors are most strongly associated with upward wage mobility among recently resettled refugees in the United States. Method: Using cross-sectional data from the 2020–2022 Annual Survey of Refugees, a nationally representative survey of recent refugees, we analyzed predictors of a binary outcome indicating whether a respondent’s current or most recent job pays a higher hourly wage than their first U.S. job. We applied machine-learning techniques to capture nonlinear relationships and interactions among predictors. Results: Descriptively, 67.7% of refugees reported higher wages than in their first U.S. job. Length of U.S. residency, gender, and age were the strongest predictors, followed by number of children, English proficiency, educational attainment, physical health, and skill-to-job match. Nonlinear patterns between predictors and wage gains were revealed, indicating that advantages in wage mobility were concentrated within specific ranges rather than increasing uniformly. Conclusions: Pre- and postmigration factors jointly shape refugees’ early economic trajectories. Findings suggest actionable implications, including strengthening early employment pathways, improving skill-to-job matching, and expanding English and job training supports to promote more equitable wage mobility during the initial years of resettlement. © 2026 Society for Social Work and Research. All rights reserved, including rights for text and data mining and training of artificial intelligence technologies or similar technologies. Published by The University of Chicago Press for the Society for Social Work and Research.},
