	affiliations = {Department of Social Work, Umeå University, Sweden; Department of Sociology, Umeå University, Sweden},
	abstract = {Immigrant women are widely portrayed as facing a serious labor market disadvantage, yet gender differences in immigrants’ occupational mobility have been little explored.The article studied gender gaps in immigrants’ occupational entrapment, defined as entering and remaining in low-skilled/ low-prestige work rather than moving on to more skilled/prestigious work.The analyses were based on longitudinal register data and followed five immigrant cohorts arriving in Sweden in the early 2000s over a 12-year period.The results show that immigrant women were more likely than men to start out in a low-skilled/low-prestige job and less likely to experience occupational mobility. However, tertiary education strongly mitigated these gender gaps. At the same time, the differences between high- and low-educated individuals widened more among women than among men. Family-related factors and country-specific human capital did not explain these patterns, but the findings indicate that occupational gender segregation can be crucial to immigrants’ mobility prospects. © 2025 Roskilde University. All rights reserved.},
