	abstract = {Elections in developing democracies often involve the politicization of social cleavages, whereas the economic dimension seems politically insignificant. How does economic development affect this state of affairs? I show that development-led economic mobility structures voting behavior around policy but can aggravate campaigning on the social cleavage. A preponderance of poor voters leads the Right to adopt a platform that is nearly identical to that of the Left and to campaign more on the social dimension. Development increases policy polarization, thereby increasing the significance of the economic dimension. However, its effect on campaigning on the social dimension depends on the nature of the social cleavage. Development promotes divisive campaigning in a primordial majority–minority cleavage and has a moderating effect only under relatively optimistic assumptions. The results provide a structural explanation for the apparent unimportance of the economic dimension in developing democracies and outline conditions under which development exacerbates the politicization of social divisions. © 2025 Southern Political Science Association. All rights reserved, including rights for text and data mining and training of artificial intelligence technologies or similar technologies. Published by The University of Chicago Press for the Southern Political Science Association.},
