	abstract = {This paper examines whether changes in households' relative income are associated with borrowing behaviour and debt burden. Using microdata from the 2018-2019 Polish Household Budget Survey, we construct a measure of Relative Income Dynamics (RID) that identifies households whose income grows faster than that of their provincial peer group. Logit estimates indicate that upward relative income movements are associated with a higher likelihood of becoming a borrower and increasing debt repayments, but with a lower likelihood of an increase in the debt-service-to-income (DSTI) ratio. These associations become weaker but remain qualitatively similar after accounting for absolute income growth. Taken together, these findings are more consistent with credit expansion under improved financial conditions than with 'keeping up with the Joneses' interpretations that link social comparisons to overborrowing and financial vulnerability. By jointly analysing borrowing, repayment behaviour, and debt burden, the paper evaluates alternative interpretations of relative income effects and provides new empirical evidence on the association between short-run relative income dynamics and household borrowing behaviour.  © 2026 The Authors.},
