	abstract = {Maintaining a high level of intergenerational income mobility is crucial for promoting social equity. In this paper, the long-term effects of free compulsory education reform in rural China on intergenerational income mobility (IGM) are evaluated. On the basis of data from the China Family Panel Studies (CFPS), we find that free education programs have a negative effect on intergenerational income elasticity (IGE) and help promote social equity. Additionally, this impact is more pronounced among disadvantaged groups or regions. The conclusion remains valid after multiple robustness tests are performed. Furthermore, mechanism analysis reveals that this program not only enhances the human capital of offspring and accelerates the accumulation of their social capital, but also facilitates their migration, improves their employment opportunities and quality, thereby promoting IGM. Our study provides empirical evidence supporting the role of free education programs in breaking the cycle of poverty across generations and offers significant insights for the design of educational poverty alleviation policies in developing countries. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
