	affiliations = {Social Sector, Inter-American Development Bank, Washington, United States; Universidad de Chile, Santiago, Chile},
	abstract = {We estimate sibling correlations in schooling (i.e., the fraction of inequality in educational outcomes attributable to factors shared by siblings) for 128 countries, which account for 94% of the world's population. With this new database, we document several findings. In the average country in our sample, at least 56% of the inequality in schooling can be attributed to factors shared by siblings, with estimated sibling correlations ranging from as low as 0.15 to as high as 0.87 across individual samples. There are significant regional differences, with Europe and Central Asia showing the lowest and South Asia the highest levels of sibling correlation. There is also substantial heterogeneity within some regions. The average sibling correlation has been decreasing across cohorts; in some regions, however, it has stagnated. At the global level, educational mobility appears strongly correlated with several social and economic variables. © 2026 The Authors},
