	abstract = {Consumers with lower social status have long demonstrated stronger preferences for material consumption (vs. experiential consumption), often becoming trapped in materialistic pursuits that diminish their well-being. Yet, little research has explored how to encourage experiential consumption among lower-status consumers effectively. In the current research, we propose that perceived economic mobility motivates low social status consumers to search for meaning, which in turn increases their preference for experiential consumption over material consumption. The results of five studies (including secondary data and experiments) confirm that perceived economic mobility shifts lower social status consumers’ preferences towards experiential consumption rather than material consumption, with this shift driven by an increased desire to search for meaning. These results deepen our understanding of the combined effect of perceived economic mobility and social status on consumers’ consumption preferences, offering valuable insights for companies to identify target markets and design effective marketing strategies. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
