	affiliations = {Department of Economics, Faculty of Economics and Administration, Masaryk University, Brno, Czech Republic},
	abstract = {Gendered constraints related to transport affordability and safety are significant barriers to women’s social and economic mobility in the Global South. In October 2019, the Government of Delhi introduced the Pink Pass policy, providing fare‑free access to public bus transport for women alongside complementary safety measures. This paper evaluates the design, implementation, and mobility outcomes of the Pink Pass policy and develops an evidence-based framework for strengthening gender-responsive fare-free transport policies. Using administrative records and primary data from the Delhi Women Survey (2023), the analysis documents patterns of mobility constraints, policy uptake, and benefits associated with fare‑free bus travel. The analysis shows that fare-free access to urban transport is associated with household savings, increased bus usage, and improved perceptions of safety, particularly among younger and lower-income women. However, these outcomes are mediated by structural constraints, including uneven policy awareness, service limitations, and fiscal sustainability concerns. This study develops a governance framework linking affordability, safety, service quality, and awareness to inform the design of urban transport policies in the Global South. The framework highlights how affordability, safety, service quality, and policy awareness interact to shape mobility outcomes and long-term policy effectiveness. © 2026 The Author(s).},
	author_keywords = {Fare-free public transport; Gender and transport; Pink Pass policy; Safety perception; Transport affordability; Urban mobility},
