	abstract = {This article analyzes how the European Union (EU) and Southern Mediterranean Countries (SMCs) have learned throughout the thirty years of the Euro-Mediterranean Partnership (EMP) in reflection of different concepts of partnership, such as privileged partnerships, advanced status, mobility partnerships, or green partnership. Focusing on stability and state building, security, energy and migration management, both on the micro and macro level, we identify learning processes within the European ‘policy community’ and illustrate how learning processes over time have resulted in changes of narratives and policies. Equally, we indicate how SMC governments have undergone political and diplomatic learning processes on their side. Building on existing scholarship, official EU material and 11 interviews with stakeholders in Brussels, we point to positive and negative examples of policy learning, applying the Special Issue’s overall typology of ‘collaboration strength’ and ‘power-sharing’ to varying learning outcomes across different policy domains and periods in the EU-SMCs relations. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Limited 2026.},
