	affiliations = {School of Social Work, University of British Columbia, BC, Canada; Department of Sociology, Trent University, ON, Canada; Political Studies department, Trent University, ON, Canada; School of Social Work and Human Services, University of the Fraser Valley, Abbotsford, BC, Canada},
	abstract = {The complexities of the labour market and related practices continue to hinder the employment integration of skilled racialized immigrants, especially women. This systematic review examines the deskilling experiences of skilled racialized immigrants through a gender and intersectional lens. It follows the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analysis (PRISMA) guidelines. Six databases were searched for peer-reviewed literature on the employment experiences of skilled racialized immigrants in Canada. Eighteen articles meeting the inclusion criteria were included in the final analysis. Content and thematic analyses of these articles showed that skilled racialized immigrants face multiple layers of deskilling that obstruct their integration into the Canadian labour market. Intersecting factors, such as the gendered division of labour at home and in the workplace, further complicate the deskilling experiences of racialized women. The findings also emphasize how limited access to family and care support influences labour market participation, particularly for women. Capitalism and racialization in the Canadian labour market further contribute to the devaluation of foreign credentials and work experience. Evidence from this review indicates that deskilling and racialization adversely affect immigrants' physical and mental health. Our review provides employers, policymakers, and service providers with intersectional insights into the post-migration deskilling experiences of racialized immigrants. Decision-makers should consider this strong evidence, alongside the ongoing othering of racialized immigrants, when creating inclusive labour market policies. Healthcare practitioners can leverage this evidence to develop targeted programs that support the mental and physical well-being of newcomers to Canada. © 2026, Canadian Ethnic Studies Association. All rights reserved.},
