	abstract = {Using micro-survey data from 17,945 respondent households in the Chinese Social Survey (CSS) for 2013–2019, this study measures land transfer intensity and evaluates common prosperity from a consumption perspective through two dimensions–‘comprehensive’ prosperity and ‘inclusive’ shared prosperity–using three indicators: consumption breadth, consumption depth, and consumption inequality. Ordinary least squares regression is utilized to quantify the effects of land transfer on common prosperity. The findings reveal: (1) As land transfer intensity increases, household consumption breadth and depth rise, while consumption inequality declines, contributing to enhancing both ‘comprehensive’ prosperity and ‘inclusive’ shared prosperity and advancing the goal of common prosperity. (2) The common prosperity effects of land transfers exhibit pronounced heterogeneity, with marked regional disparities observed across China’s eastern, central, and western regions, as well as between northern and southern regions. Following the ‘three rights separation’ reform of the rural land market, accelerating the transfer of land rights has become more conducive to achieving common prosperity. (3) Occupational mobility and income stability may be potential mechanisms underlying this relationship. Land transfer enhances occupational mobility and stabilizes rural household income, thereby affecting the breadth and depth of consumption as well as consumption inequality, and ultimately promoting the realization of common prosperity. © 2026 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
