	abstract = {This paper investigates the role of export shocks in promoting intergenerational income mobility in China by the data of CHIP. Export expansion has different impacts on different regions and industries, which in turn leads to the migration of workers to places with more export opportunities. During the process of migration, individuals reassess and alter their occupational and educational choices. Such migration and corresponding changes in career and education paths have shifted income rankings between parents and their children, thereby enhancing intergenerational income mobility. Furthermore, the impact of export shocks on income mobility varies across hukou status, genders, industrial sectors, and regions. Our findings suggest that reducing the restrictions of hukou and facilitating labour mobility could help mitigate socioeconomic stratification. Strengthening trade relations with the Middle East and Southeast Asia may play a vital role in boosting China’s trade volumes and improving labour incomes. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
