	affiliations = {Yunnan University, China; Hazara University, Pakistan},
	abstract = {This paper examines transnational networks of Pakistani migrants in two Chinese cities, Guangzhou and Shaoxing. It investigates how five key migrant networks (interpersonal, caste-based, religious, ethnic, and social networks) collectively influence three fundamental aspects of Pakistani everyday life in China: collaboration, conflict, and integration. Interpersonal networks comprising family and friendship ties are pivotal in fostering collaboration by providing critical support for settlement in China and strengthening economic mobility; ethnic networks strengthen mutual collaboration for new migrants, enhance economic cooperation, and help preserve cultural identity; religious networks offer a platform for spiritual activities and material assistance; caste-based networks generate solidarity through collaboration and transnational networking; and social organizations help bridge internal differences among migrants and facilitate broader integration into and cooperation with Chinese society. However, due to the exclusivity of these networks, particularly ethnic and religious networks, conflicts can arise among migrants, thereby reducing the level of integration into Chinese society. This has led to a reliance on a traditional dispute resolution mechanism in Pakistan, the Jirga. This study contributes to broader discussions on diasporas, migrant networks, international migration to China, and the multifaceted nature of South-South migration in a globalizing world. © 2025 BCAS, Inc.},
