	affiliations = {Faculty of Education, Shaanxi Normal University, Xi’an, China; Business School, Beijing Normal University, Beijing, China},
	abstract = {This study examines the long-term impact of primary school quality–proxied by the teacher-student ratio–on adult earnings in China, using nationally representative micro-data from the 2018 China Household Income Project matched with provincial education statistics. To reinforce identification, we employ both external and internal IV approaches. To assess robustness, we further relax IV validity assumptions using plausibly exogenous and imperfect IV approaches, and estimate a multilevel mixed-effects model to account for the hierarchical structure of the data. The results consistently indicate that higher school quality causally increases adult earnings, primarily by extending educational attainment and enhancing returns to education. Notably, the positive effects are significantly stronger among females, rural residents, and individuals from less-educated family backgrounds. Additionally, larger returns observed in older cohorts highlight that benefits from improved school quality accumulate substantially over the life course. These findings offer fresh insights into the pivotal role of early education investment in shaping labour market outcomes and enhancing intergenerational mobility in developing contexts. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
