	affiliations = {Columbia University, United States},
	abstract = {This article reads Tomás Rivera's formal innovations in …y no se lo tragó la tierra (1971) to address the dual nature of the “arrival”—geographic and abstract/economic—and how an evergreen aspiration becomes economically productive for a number of other actors, even as the nature of labor changes. I use Rivera's attention to time and circularity and Mikhail Bakhtin's notion of the chronotope to explore Rivera's depiction of the relationship between the ideological power of the desire for upward economic mobility and the generation of consent and compliance to exploitative relations of debt. I argue that in exposing a foundational piece of the logic of the American Dream, he shows a key way in which the mechanism of debt operates; he shows how the accumulation of capital and linear narratives of the progress of some individuals both elide and extract value from the fraught, contested, and yet seemingly inescapable experience of temporal cyclicality by others. In sum, this literary investigation attends to the formal negotiation of the experience of time and space in Tierra to offer a better understanding of indebted subjectivity. © 2023 Society for the Study of the Multi-Ethnic Literature of the United States. All rights reserved.},
