	affiliations = {Department of Education, Tel-Hai Academic College, Kiryat Shmona, Upper Galilee, Israel},
	abstract = {This study examined the effects of higher education intervention programs on the social and occupational mobility of single mothers in Israel. Single-parent households are more likely to suffer from poverty than a family with two members, with single mothers being more vulnerable to poverty than single fathers. Because this impacts the household members in the present and their quality of life in the future, reducing poverty and promoting social and occupational mobility among single-mother families are important policy goals, which could be achieved through higher education. This article examines the outcomes of the Maskila Program, a higher education program for single mothers in Israel, on their social and occupational mobility. A mixed-method study was used to gather quantitative data through questionnaires and qualitative data through in-depth interviews. The findings indicate that the program has had a positive impact on employment, with more participants being employed and working in more stable, higher skilled positions. The program has also had a positive impact on income and job satisfaction. It was found that job satisfaction is higher among the program graduates, and both the current and the past Maskila participants presented higher levels of self-ability and self-esteem of professional capabilities compared with other participants. As such, the Maskila Program was found to contribute to occupational mobility but not to social mobility. Policymakers and key opinion leaders should address the related poverty trap when defining the social benefits' threshold for single mothers. © Common Ground Research Networks, Raviv Anat, All Rights Reserved.},
