	abstract = {The objective of this paper is to examine patterns in the relationship between intergenerational class mobility and family structure, especially number of siblings or birth order, using SSM cumulative data. We applied the Core Model, a kind of log-linear model for the Japanese mobility table, and arrived at several findings as follows. First, we analyzed the relationship between class origins and family structure and family structure and class destinations. The disparity in fertility levels based on class origins has been stable. Besides, the association between family structure and class destinations has also been stable over time among male siblings. Moreover, differences in intergenerational class mobility pattern due to birth order were observed only among male. The chances of mobility of a respondent are lower if he was born earlier than other siblings. In addition, the structure of intergenerational class mobility is stable among people born between the two birth cohorts. However, differences in the mobility patterns among male siblings become equalized in the case of self-employed class, but become un-equalized in the case of farming class. The results show that the effect of family structure on intergenerational social mobility can reduce due to social changes such as birthrate decline. © 2018 Japanese Association for Mathematical Sociology. All rights reserved.},
