	affiliations = {Department of Anthropology, University of Montreal, P.O. Box 6128, Succursale Centreville, Montreal, H3C 3J7, QC, Canada},
	abstract = {Social universals are panhuman categories containing a large number of distinctcultural forms that share motivational and functional commonalities. Very little isknown about the origins of those culturally polymorphic categories and how they relateto psychological universals. To address that question, one must first identify the mostelementary of social universals-individual social acts, the likely building blocks ofmore complex ones. On theoretical grounds, psychological adaptations are expected toplay a central role in the production of individual social acts. Psychological adaptationshave been hypothesized to regulate behavior in 2 different ways: by evoking distinct, genetically specified behavioral outputs in different socioecological contexts, and byimposing some sort of constraints on cultural transmission. How those processes relateto social universals is largely unknown. The present model has a pyramidal structureand rests on 4 propositions: (1) achieving a psychological adaptation's functionalobjective involves achieving a specific number of subgoals, or systemic social motives;(2) these are elicited in a number of universal categories of socioecological contextsexhibiting the relevant cues (3) the resulting context-specific motives translate into anumber of cognitively different, internally generated universal social propensities; (4)the latter in turn bias the mechanisms of creativity and social learning in producingpropensity-congruent cultural variants (or culturally polymorphic categories of socialacts). The model is exemplified with 2 adaptations-status attainment and fidelitycontrol, which comprise a minimum of 40 and 16 propensities, respectively. Universalpropensities would embody the very foundation of the sociocultural realm. © 2016 American Psychological Association.},
