	abstract = {This paper examines the mechanism of entry into self-employment from the viewpoints of unemployment rate and inter/intra-generational mobility in Japan. Previous studies have argued that an increase in self-employment reduces the unemployed by focusing on the relationship between the change in unemployment rate and number of self-employed people. On the other hand, the individual factors determining entry into self-employment are explored from the viewpoints of inter-generational mobility of self-employment and their previous work experiences. However, there are very few studies integrating those approaches of both macro and micro levels in analyzing the mechanism of how entry into self-employment unfolds. Using the unemployment data and the 2005 Social Stratification and Mobility survey data, we conduct the analysis with the discrete-time logit model and clarified the following points. The result shows that there is no relationship between unemployment rate and mobility into blue-collar self-employment, while the mobility into white-collar self-employment is suppressed as the unemployment rate rises up. Although previous studies pointed out that work experiences in small and medium-sized companies facilitate the entry into to self-employment, our studies reveals that those experiences do not foster the entry into white-collar self-employment as unemployment rate goes up. © 2014, Japanese Association for Mathematical Sociology. All rights reserved.},
