	affiliations = {Pôle Hospitalo-Universitaire de Gérontologie Clinique, CHU Nantes, Nantes, France; Gérontopole, Service de Santé Publique, CHU de Toulouse, Toulouse, France; INSERM 1027, Toulouse, France; Department of Gender, Women and Health, World Health Organization (WHO), Geneva, Switzerland; Department of Geriatric Medicine, Damiaan General Hospital, Ostend, Belgium; Department of Family Medicine, Mc Gill University, Montreal, Canada; Department of Geriatrics, Nelson R. Mandela School of Medicine, University of KwaZulu-Natal, Durban, South Africa; European Medicines Agency (EMA), London, United Kingdom; Department of Social Welfare, Hallym University, Chuncheon, Gangwon-do, South Korea; Center for Geriatrics and Gerontology, Taipei Veterans General Hospital, Taipei, Taiwan; Geriatrics, IRCCS-INRCA, Ancona, Italy; Institute of Gerontology and Geriatrics, Department of Clinical and Experimental Medicine, University of Perugia Medical School, Perrugia, Italy; Geriatric Medicine Center, Kaohsiung Veterans General Hospital, Kaohsiung City, Taiwan; Geriatric Department, Hospital Universitario Ramón y Cajal, Madrid, Spain; Academic Research Office, Remnin University, Beijing, China; Hôpital Sainte Perrine, Paris, France; ILC-International Longevity Center, Paris, France; Département de Médecine Gériatrique, CHU de Nice, Nice, France; School of Health Sciences, Brasília, Brazil; Instituto Nacional de Geriatría, Mexico City, Mexico; Hospital Italiano, Buenos Aires, Argentina; St.-Petersburg Institute of Bioregulation and Gerontology, St.-Petersburg, Russian Federation; Division of Geriatric Medicine, Taipei Veterans General Hospital, Yuanshan Branch, I-Lan, Taiwan; Division of Geriatric Medicine, Center for Geriatrics and Gerontology, Taipei Veterans General Hospital, Taipei, Taiwan; Département de Médecine Gériatrique, CHU de Nancy, 54511 Vandoeuvre-les-Nancy, Avenue de Bourgogne BP 217, France; Department of Geriatrics, Ghent University Hospital, Ghent, Belgium; Centre Gérontologique Départemental, Marseille, France; Department of Medicine, Dalhousie University, Halifax, NS, Canada; Department of Geriatrics, Hospital Universitario de Getafe, Madrid, Spain; Friedrich-Alexander-University, Erlangen-Nuernberg, Germany; Public Primary Health Center of Itea, Phokida, Greece; Department of Geriatrics, First Faculty of Medicine, Charles University, Prague, Czech Republic; Gérontopôle, Service de Médecine Interne Gériatrique, CHU Toulouse, Toulouse, France},
	abstract = {Frailty tends to be considered as a major risk for adverse outcomes in older persons, but some important aspects remain matter of debate. Objectives: The purpose of this paper is to present expert's positions on the main aspects of the frailty syndrome in the older persons. Participants: Workshop organized by International Association of Gerontology and Geriatrics (IAGG), World Health Organization (WHO) and Société Française de Gériatrie et de Gérontologie (SFGG). Results: Frailty is widely recognized as an important risk factor for adverse health outcomes in older persons. This can be of particular value in evaluating non-disabled older persons with chronic diseases but today no operational definition has been established. Nutritional status, mobility, activity, strength, endurance, cognition, and mood have been proposed as markers of frailty. Another approach calculates a multidimensional score ranging from "very fit" to "severely frail," but it is difficult to apply into the medical practice. Frailty appears to be secondary to multiple conditions using multiple pathways leading to a vulnerability to a stressor. Biological (inflammation, loss of hormones), clinical (sarcopenia, osteoporosis etc.), as well as social factors (isolation, financial situation) are involved in the vulnerability process. In clinical practice, detection of frailty is of major interest in oncology because of the high prevalence of cancer in older persons and the bad tolerance of the drug therapies. Presence of frailty should also be taken into account in the definition of the cardiovascular risks in the older population. The experts of the workshop have listed the points reached an agreement and those must to be a priority for improving understanding and use of frailty syndrome in practice. Conclusion: Frailty in older adults is a syndrome corresponding to a vulnerability to a stressor. Diagnostic tools have been developed but none can integrate at the same time the large spectrum of factors and the simplicity asked by the clinical practice. An agreement with an international common definition is necessary to develop screening and to reduce the morbidity in older persons. © 2013 Serdi and Springer-Verlag France.},
	author_keywords = {diagnostic and workshop; frailty; Older persons; vulnerability},
