	affiliations = {La population du Saguenay-Lac-Saint-Jean, Groupe de recherche interdisciplinaire en demographie et epidemiologie genetique, Université du Québec, Chicoutimi, Canada},
	abstract = {Occupational mobility is defined as a transition from one occupation to another. Different angles can be used to shed light on this phenomenon either over the life course of an individual or across successive generations. This study relies on an inter-generational approach with an emphasis on sibship characteristics: presence of brothers and sisters, birth ranks, number of married sibs. Analyses were undertaken using data from the BALSAC Register on the Saguenay-Lac-St-Jean (Quebec, Canada) population of the XIX<sup>th</sup> and XX<sup>th</sup> centuries. The main purpose of the study was to measure the impact of sibship characteristics on the probability for an individual to have an occupation different from his father's. The analysis was based on the comparison of fathers' and sons' occupations at the time of their first marriage, which corresponds in most instances to the beginning of adulthood. Occupations were grouped into four broad categories: farmers, semi-skilled and unskilled workers, skilled workers and craftsmen, white collars. Results show that 53% of the subjects had an occupation different from their father's and that this proportion increases with the period of marriage. Before 1940, farmers' sons had a lower occupational mobility than other sons. Of the six sibship characteristics that were investigated, five have a statistically significant effect on occupational mobility although the explicative value of these effects is small. Sibship size has a negative effect, and number of married brothers has the strongest impact. Birth rank, however, acts positively on mobility and this impact is higher for birth rank among brothers. Hence, it appears relevant to take sibship characteristics into account when building explicative models of occupational mobility.},
	publisher = {Editions Belin et Herscher},
