	year = {2010},
	journal = {Desarrollo Economico},
	volume = {49},
	number = {196},
	pages = {573 - 604},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/79951891687?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {CONICET, Argentina; CEDOP-UBA, Argentina; Instituto Gino Germani, Caba (1114), Uriburu 950, 60 24, Argentina},
	abstract = {This paper tries to extend to Argentina the exploration of the hypothesis of persistent educational inequality, studied in several industrialized countries. The aim is to evaluate the degree of association between socio-educational origins and educational achievements. Data from a 2007 Argentinian national mobility survey are used Several approaches are proposed and analysed. First, the number of completed years of education are taken as dependent variables in multiple linear regressions, while social and educational background are considered as independent variables (father and/or mother education, father socioeconomic status or class of father, etc.). Given the limitations attributed to linear regression for this type of study, logistic regressions are used, as well as conditional logistic regression, which consider the chances of achieving a higher educational level after completing the previous educational level. Finally, a third approach is to consider educational intergenerational mobility by means of traditional mobility tables and models. The models explored are those of conditional independence, constant association and unifform differences. The traditional regression analysis shows similar results that those for Industrialized countries: social and educational backgound variables decline their influence on educational achievements. When condicional logistic regressions are considered, for example to achieve the university level given de secondary level, the high educational level of origins are not important for educational achievement of older people, while father social class effectes are less defined. The educational mobility analysis shows that the constant association model produces the best fit, although the uniform differences model shows no variations in educational mobility thorough cohorts, although it suggests higher educational mobility for women. No clear pattern is observed, with mixed results about increasing or decreasing inequality of educational opportunities.},
