	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84859079706?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Since the take-off of the economy in the early 1970s, the idea of "you can succeed through your own efforts" has dominated the mainstream discourse in Hong Kong. Class-related discussions in the territory are mainly concerned with upward social mobility and the so-called "middle class dream". The influence of class origin on social mobility has been neglected while education has been generally viewed as the key incentive and instrument for upward social mobility. Early quantitative studies mainly focus on the magnitude and path of inter-generational mobility, the openness of the social structure, and the political orientation of the middle class (Wong and Lui, 1992; Lui and Wong, 1994; Chan, Lui and Wong, 1995; Tsang, 1992). Recent studies mainly discuss the relationship among social mobility, family background, education level and Mainland-Hong Kong integration (Lui, 2009; Wu, 2009). After Hong Kong's return to China, on one hand, there is the worry of the "decline" of the middle class in the economic downturn. On the other hand, the "Hong Kong spirit" was advocated to emphasize that younger generation could improve their social status with their own efforts. The purpose of this study is to understand the perception of upward mobility and the path for improving social status among the "Fourth Generation of Hong Kong" (those who were born between 1976 and 1990), as well as their evaluation of the relationship between individual efforts and "success".},
