	abstract = {An intergenerational male occupational mobility table of Buenos Aires City (1982) is analyzed, and comparisons with a similar one from the Buenos Aires Metropolitan Area (1969) are introduced. Discussions take place within a context of hypotheses on the invariance of mobility patterns in industrial or industrializing societies and of hypotheses on the existence of 'closure' in the higher levels and/or 'buffer-zones' in the middle levels of the occupational or class structure. On the basis of theoretical and pragmatic concerns, four occupational or class categories are used (high and low non manual and manual). Models of perfect and quasi-perfect mobility do not fit the data, so a version of the corners model is considered and it achieves an acceptable fit. If immobility due to occupational inheritance (main diagonal) is excluded and the 'excess' short-distance mobility in the nonmanual stratum (Buenos Aires City), or the 'excess' short-distance mobility in the manual stratum (Metropolitan Area) are also excluded - situations where father influences still persit-, perfect mobility is observed in the rest of each table. Rigidity found in the manual stratum of 1969 would be present in the nonmanual statum thirteen years later. Findings tend to question hypotheses on the existence of 'closure' or of a 'buffer-zone', and to lend suport to hypotheses on the invariance of mobility patterns.},
