	affiliations = {University of Memphis, Department of Anthropology, Memphis, TN, United States},
	abstract = {This paper illustrates how one community interpreted and acted upon the interlocking systems of oppression (corporate greed, institutional racism, and uneven economic opportunity) which converged upon their neighborhood in the form of toxic contamination. Rather than analyzing this case as an instance where a community viewed a local industry as suddenly moving from provider to poisoner, I examine the ways that community relationships with local industry changed over several decades, revealing the complexity of such situations. I find that relationships between communities and industry, even after an industry has contaminated a community, are ever in flux. Moreover, I explore the shifting and ambiguous role of state institutions in mediating community/ industry conflicts: sometimes intervening on a community's behalf and sometimes facilitating corporate interests. I conclude by addressing recent cleavages in neighborhood solidarity, which can be attributed to uneven class mobility. I find that although initially, racial identity acted as a primary basis for activism, class divisions eventually became yet another factor complicating and inhibiting the search for environmental justice. Thus, this paper demonstrates how long-term, nuanced and complex ethnographic analysis can provide a deeper understanding of the multiplicity of factors involved in environmental justice struggles. © 2005 The Institute, Inc.},
