	affiliations = {Coordinator of Pension Reform Unit, Russian Foundation for Social Reform, RECEP, Russian Federation},
	abstract = {Even under conditions of statutory constraints, effective contraction of the workforce went ahead intensively, as is proved by the above occupational mobility indicators. We believe that in the first stage the reductions took the distorted form of crowding out of the workforce from enterprises. Such practices as widespread and lengthy wage arrears, involuntary leave, in-kind payment of wages, etc., became widespread and encouraged the most mobile, constructively minded workers to quit. In other words, workforce release took the form of forced labour turnover with workers quitting voluntarily. In the 1990s such practices accounted for two-thirds (up to 80% in some industries) of all severance, allowing management to reduce staff in a relatively painless way through natural attrition without mass workforce cuts (Chizhova). Tough legislative constraints that failed to do justice to the real situation thus led Russian management to design a specific model of workforce reduction - forced labour turnover. Apparently, in the first stages of reform, under conditions of tough budgetary constraints at the federal level, macroeconomic instability and, most important, the deplorable financial situation of most enterprises, the model was adequate. However, this model of workforce reduction is characterised by very high social and economic costs.},
