	affiliations = {The Bartlett School of Planning, University College London, London WC1H 0QB, 22 Gordon Street, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {Over the past century, various terminologies have been used to define an increasing isolated mass of impoverished people, whose chances of upward social and economic mobility are minimal. The Victorians and their American counterparts called them the vicious, the degenerate and semi-criminal classes. The more enlightened late-twentieth century calls them the disadvantaged and the underprivileged (Hall, 1994). More recently, the term underclass has been used, both sociologically and in public life, to refer to this persistent phenomenon. Further, within the European Union, the term social exclusion has been given a central place in the discussion of social inequalities and social policies in Europe. There is frequent reference to the decline in social cohesion and social solidarity and the need to reintegrate the socially excluded into mainstream society. While the phrase is a newcomer to Britain, it now peppers academics' and politicians' speeches frequently. The Labour administration has even launched a special 'Social Exclusion Unit' to combat the problem. While the concept provides useful discursive aspects, by focusing on both structural and cultural/social processes, the precise meaning of social exclusion remains elusive. Nor is it easy to find systematic applications of the concept in research irrespective of its frequent use in the academic and policy literature. The first part of this review paper attempts to provide a clearer understanding of the concept of social exclusion, its origin and its multiple dimensions. It highlights deficiencies with social exclusion in terms of its analytical power and argues that without a clear theoretical understanding of the causal processes producing exclusion, the concept of social exclusion could follow the same path which has been taken by the 'underclass' debate in the USA. The second part of the paper reviews the ways in which the British planning system has addressed the concept of social exclusion in both its processes and its outcomes.},
