	abstract = {The model of core social fluidity in industrial societies proposed in Part I is briefly reviewed, and is then applied to intergenerational mobility tables for nine European nations. Significant deviations from the model are in most cases apparent, but these are not large and some support may be claimed for the 'FJH hypothe sis' in its weaker version: i.e. if understood as claiming that fluidity patterns display a large cross-national commonality. For those nations where the core model does not fit satisfactorily, variant forms are proposed and their implications considered. It is argued that such variation in fluidity is the more damaging to the FJH hypothesis, even in its weaker form, to the extent that it can be accounted for in terms of macro-sociological variables-rather than requiring appeal to nationally-specific factors. The most important such variable would appear to be that of political intervention; but its explanatory power is qualified since intervention may be directed to differing goals and with differing degrees of success. © 1987 Oxford University Press.},
