	abstract = {Aims to demonstrate that different patterns of spatial and economic mobility exist which require a number of concurrent strategies to harness the talents of urban-bound migrants. Through examination of individual migration histories, the analyses shows that few migrants are life time rural-to-urban movers. Most continue to move up and down the urban hierarchy as they pass through the stages of their life cycles. This suggests that migrants born in rural locales can make their first move as a child of a migrant or as a young adult and can continue to move among urban centres and to and from rural locales. More important, the analyses shows that urban natives are highly mobile and represent in some cities more than half of the migrant pool. Moreover, most tend to be repeat movers who circulate among urban centres to improve their socioeconomic status. Also demonstrates that the numerous spatial patterns of mobility are accompanied by the transfer of a diverse mixture of occupational skills and training, influenced by the migrants' spatial behaviour. Multiple migrants and interurban movers tend to arrive in urban centres with high levels of training and occupational skills to aid in the growth and development of the urban economy.-from Author},
