	abstract = {Objective. The purpose of this research is to explore the impact of Catholic religious affiliation on the socioeconomic status of black Americans. Attempts are made to specify the role of racial integration and conversion to Catholicism as factors shaping the biographies of black Catholics. Methods. A secondary analysis of a recent nationwide sample of black Americans is used to explore secular status differences between black Protestants and black Catholics. Regression analyses examine the consequences of religious affiliation for socioeconomic differences, controlling for a variety of background, demographic, and contextual factors. Results. Analyses show both regional and generational patterns of distinctiveness of black Catholics. While there are no nationwide differences in status by religious affiliation, black Catholics are more likely to attend racially integrated churches in all regions and do have higher socioeconomic status in the North. This status advantage is linked to some distinctive consequences of conversion to Catholicism for different generations. Among older black Catholics, it is those who convert to Catholicism who have higher status; among younger black Catholics, it is those raised in Catholic families who have higher status. Conclusions. Although conversion to Catholicism was an important component of status attainment for earlier generations of black Americans, it no longer has a decisive effect. In recent decades, Catholic affiliation is predictive of higher status only among those blacks born into Catholic families. These findings suggest that it is the consolidation of a previous generation's status gains that shapes this new type of Catholic status advantage.},
