	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85040894143?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Does expanding the secondary education system make good economic sense for a developing country, and what are its effects on income differentials and intergenerational mobility? Investigates these issues with reference to the "natural experiment' of Kenya and Tanzania. Similar in many ways - size, colonial background, natural resources, economic structure - but with divergent educational policies (secondary enrollment is much higher in Kenya) these two countries offer an opportunity to test some of the key policy questions concerning secondary education. The authors gathered information on urban workers and tested cognitive skill and reasoning ability to find links between the availability of secondary education and productivity. The volume is in six parts: introduction and overview; educational expansion and labour productivity; educational expansion, government policy, and the structure and dispersion of pay; educational expansion and inequality of opportunity; the cost benefit analysis of secondary education, and conclusions. The authors conclude that secondary education does yield a high rate of return, that it contributes substantially to economic growth and reduces inequalities in pay through compressing the educational structure of wages, but that it is also unlikely to improve the relative position of the poor in the socioeconomic hierarchy. Appendices give details on survey design, questionnaire and test material and other background information. -M.Amos},
