	abstract = {A large number of professorial appointments in the 1970s and a simultaneous unusually low professor age caused an overflow situation. In the 1990s this has caused a high professor age, indicating that more than half of all professors will have to be replaced by 2007. This need for personnel moves professor recruitment to the forefront of personnel politics and scientific research. Direct and indirect regional recruitment indicate the level of openness or the provinciality of universities. The regional recruiting area of Baden-Wurttemberg's universities becomes smaller with each increasing career stage. This spatial concentration is particularly high in engineering, while it is much lower for law, business and the social sciences. Spatial mobility is a key aspect of a higher education career in German-speaking areas, but it is comparably less important due to the faculty inbreeding at the time of personnel expansion. The high personnel need of a generation switch will affect the spatial mobility behaviour of professors, and their inter- and intragenerational mobility. As a result, social groups that were not recognized before will be considered for recruitment. The future of old, technical and new, centrally and peripherally located universities depends on how they handle the generation switch to new, younger staff. The scientific quality and the productivity and attractiveness of the universities depends on their flexibility. This flexibility is a key element in the arrangement of the institutes, the faculties and the entire higher educational system on both regional and national levels. -English summary},
