	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84958309960?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {It is a pity that T. Shanin (1973) felt that my review (1973) contained no relevant criticism, and that it contained ‘personal side-slaps’ and so on. To deal with the latter point first, I do not hold people responsible for the theoretical effects of the concepts they use. These effects are produced not by the intentions of the author but by the concepts themselves and the problematic at work in his text. Consequently my criticisms of The Awkward Class (1972) were in no way directed personally at Shanin. Indeed, many of the features of the book which I would criticise are very widespread insociological texts. On the former point, the main reason that Shanin considers that the review was largely innocent of the text reviewed or else irrelevant to the issues concerned is that he has not appreciated what kind of a review it was. It was not the kind of review referred to by K. Williams (1972: 457-458)’ which is primarily concerned to elucidate and assess revisions in the text reviewed, that is,arguments for the inversion or qualification of accepted judgments on issuesdefined by the predominant theoretical discourse. It was a theoretical and methodological review which attempted to question the issues themselves, and to elucidate and criticise the problematic which produced them as issues. Shanin's reply is not directed at the gaps and unevenness of this critique, but rather at what seems to him to be its failure to take account of certain parts of the The Awkward Class which say the ‘exact opposite’ to the review's ‘exposition’ of the book. Heis confirmed in his impression by the fact that the review is misleading on several minor points. I am grateful to him for drawing my attention to these points, and shall indicate them at the appropriate places,but the bulk of the reply will consist of an attempt to briefly indicate how Shanin's reading of the review is an effect of the same problematic which produced the discourse of the book. Since many of his comments are based on a misreading of the review it is unnecessary to reply to all of them. In what follows I shall reply in detail to his comments on Socio-Economic Mobility, and on the other two sections, restrict my remarks to the more substantial points. I shall follow Shanin's numbering throughout. © 1973, Taylor & Francis Group, LLC. All rights reserved.},
