	abstract = {Studies of intergenerational occupational mobility in four urban industrial societies which reported data on more than one son per family, are reviewed to support the following generalizations about the relations of status, mobility, and education in such societies: variance of status by families as contrasted with variance of status between individuals independent of their family membership comprises a substantial percent of the total variance of status; variance of mobility by families (“sibling mobility”) is similarly substantial; and the effect of education on mobility is more to generate sibling mobilty than to make individuals mobile independent of their membership in a particular family. This effect of education on mobility is interpreted as evidence that education is more of a source of privilege than a genuinely egalitarian influence in society, more of a means by which status is ascribed than a means by which status is achieved. © 1975, Sage Publications. All rights reserved.},
