	affiliations = {Dartmouth College, Harvard University, United States},
	abstract = {An abstract model is developed for the relation between social distance, measured on an interval scale, and social mobility. Applied to British mobility data the model is used to analyze occupational status mobility, specifically, to analyze the pattern by which the occupational status of a son is related to the occupational status of his father. There appear to be two components to the interaction among statuses. The first component places a son near his father's absolute position, whereas the second component places a son near his father's relative position. The abstract model proposes an exact description of these two effects, and the description fits the British data. The model is substantively and methodologically close to several earlier mobility analyses, but it is shown to be more parsimonious and more accurate, and the model suggests that the actual social process underlying the mobility data may be more regular and more well‐behaved than had been anticipated. Copyright © 1972 John Wiley & Sons, Ltd.},
