	abstract = {The welfare workers involved in the original study were sought out after an interval of 3.5 yr to determine the extent to which they had achieved professionalization (as identified with the Master of Social Work degree) and the extent to which they were mobile in their work. A reflection of the problem was the fact that only 55% of the original group could be reached. A range of mobility was established, from those on education or maternity leave but committed to return to the agency, to those with other agencies or in related jobs, to those who had left the field altogether. Internal mobility was found to respond to peer influence, while all other forms of mobility involved some form of dissatisfaction with client worker relations, although the workers never stated this in giving reasons for leaving their jobs. High levels of strain were noted in relation to working conditions, relations with  the agency, etc., but none of these areas actively influenced mobility. The author concludes that better recruitment will do little to solve the mobility problems, and that agencies should be experimenting with changes in function in order to reduce debilitating mobility.},
